WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.560
Part 3. You will hear two students, called Rosie and Colin, discussing their Human Geography

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:15.320
assignment. First, you have some time to look at questions 21 to 25.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:53.840
Colin, I'm really struggling to think of a topic for our Human Geography assignment.

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:59.840
Me too, Rosie. I'll tell you what, let's think about the different aspects of Human Geography

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:03.599
and see if we can narrow the topic down a bit to help us decide.

00:01:03.599 --> 00:01:09.360
Okay, so one aspect is population. That would be all about population density and migration

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:13.760
and so on. Lots of facts and statistics. Maybe a bit boring?

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:17.440
Yeah, but quite straightforward to find on the internet.

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:23.199
Suppose so. How about health? I'd never thought about the links between that and geography

00:01:23.199 --> 00:01:28.400
until Professor Lee gave us that lecture on cholera, how in the 19th century a physician

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:33.440
used street plans and plans of water supplies to find the source of a cholera epidemic.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:40.160
Yes, fascinating, wasn't it? Or we could do something more general, like economies.

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:43.919
So how financial and commercial factors are linked to the physical environment?

00:01:43.919 --> 00:01:45.760
Yeah. I thought that had been taken

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:48.080
off the syllabus for this year. Has it?

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:50.959
I'm not sure, but it might be best to avoid it.

00:01:50.959 --> 00:01:58.160
Okay. Maybe we could do something on culture. We had that lecture about culture and geography

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:00.800
last week. I didn't get much out of that.

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:05.040
It was all so general and the lecturer didn't give any useful examples.

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:08.639
Yeah, I hardly took any notes. It didn't seem worth it.

00:02:08.639 --> 00:02:11.919
Me neither. We could focus on poverty.

00:02:11.919 --> 00:02:16.559
That's something that's a global problem. The trouble is, Dr Lee was saying, that you

00:02:16.559 --> 00:02:19.759
have to be careful with some of the figures relating to poverty.

00:02:19.759 --> 00:02:24.559
They're sometimes deliberately manipulated. You mean the information gets changed for

00:02:24.559 --> 00:02:26.960
political reasons? That sort of thing, yes.

00:02:28.479 --> 00:02:30.479
Okay.

00:02:30.479 --> 00:02:36.479
Before you hear the rest of the discussion, you have some time to look at questions 26-30.

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:24.320
So what are we going to do our assignment on? I'll tell you what. One of the possibilities

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:29.520
we haven't discussed is urbanisation. And now over half the world's population lives

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:34.960
in cities. That's really important. Okay, good idea, Rosie. Let's do that.

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:38.320
I love living in a big city, but of course there are problems.

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:43.039
Things like theft and robbery? Yes, but where I come from, that's linked

00:03:43.039 --> 00:03:48.240
to another more serious issue, which is that a lot of people don't have jobs. That's getting

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:53.199
worse and worse. And we also still have quite a lot of people who are homeless, though that's

00:03:53.199 --> 00:03:57.199
not quite so bad as it was. It'd be nice to talk about some positive

00:03:57.199 --> 00:04:02.399
developments, like some of the new developments on the outskirts of cities.

00:04:02.399 --> 00:04:06.320
Yeah, they've opened some massive new shopping centres outside my city.

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:11.520
Yeah, the same with mine. But it's meant a lot of the shops in the city centre are closing

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:18.320
down. But the outskirts are ideal for buildings that need a lot of space, like for conferences.

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:21.519
They've opened a couple of big ones. Yes, we've got some too.

00:04:22.239 --> 00:04:26.160
It'd be nicer to have more facilities for things like football too,

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:28.959
but that's not happening where I live. Same in my area.

00:04:29.519 --> 00:04:33.600
We could include something about developing disused industrial sites.

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:38.239
It seems like a good idea because you're not doing any harm to the natural environment.

00:04:38.239 --> 00:04:42.399
Yeah, but aren't the buildings architecturally significant?

00:04:42.399 --> 00:04:47.679
Not really. And what people forget is that they often used quite dangerous materials,

00:04:47.679 --> 00:04:51.040
chemicals and things, which haven't been properly cleared away.

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:55.279
So the whole site has to be made safe. Hmm, that can't be cheap.

00:04:55.279 --> 00:04:57.920
And I bet it's often not budgeted for. You're right.

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:01.920
Have you read about Masdar City? In Abu Dhabi?

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:05.119
Yes. It was designed to be a green city, wasn't it?

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:10.880
That might be a good example of a city which set out to depend entirely on renewable energy.

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:16.959
Yes, we should say something about that. It was designed to be totally pedestrianised

00:05:16.959 --> 00:05:20.160
too, wasn't it? With the transport underground.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:25.119
Yes, and they had big plans for recycling to reduce waste to the lowest possible level.

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:27.839
But let's stick to talking about power sources.

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:32.640
Then there's that eco-town in England, Greenhill Abbotts.

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:38.079
It set out to conform to the usual principles, sustainability and so on.

00:05:38.079 --> 00:05:42.799
A lot of people were against it at first. They said the plans were unrealistic.

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:47.679
I'm not sure how far they've got with it. I'll check.

00:05:47.679 --> 00:05:50.399
Right. So it looks as if we have a sort of plan.

00:05:54.880 --> 00:06:02.640
That is the end of part three. You now have 30 seconds to check your answers to part three.

00:06:12.799 --> 00:06:13.299
Thank you.

